---
layout: layouts/base.njk
title: Bible Carrel
description: Bible Carrel is a native app for Mac and iPhone for reading through the Bible with highlights, notes, bookmarks and a study assistant.
---
{#- Every picture is named here; a missing file fails the build. The "Yours to keep" row is words alone, set as a centered coda. #}
{%- set heroSizes = "(min-width: 65rem) 60rem, 100vw" %}
{%- set rowSizes = "(min-width: 800px) 36rem, 100vw" %}
{%- set phoneSizes = "(min-width: 800px) 10rem, 33vw" %}
<div class="home">
  <div class="title-block">
    <p class="eyebrow">For Mac and iPhone</p>
    <h1>Bible Carrel</h1>
    <p class="lede">A quiet desk for reading through the Bible: the page set like print, your highlights and notes in its margin, and a study assistant at your elbow.</p>
    <p class="title-links">
      <a class="button" href="{{ '/build/' | url }}">Build it from source</a>
      <a href="{{ '/guide/first-chapter/' | url }}">Read the guide ›</a>
    </p>
    <p class="small">Not in the App Store and not downloadable. You build it yourself from its source, with Xcode.</p>
    <div class="hero-shot">
      {% shot "reader-hero", "The reader on John 1 in the Printed style, opening on the book's title, with one phrase highlighted in yellow and the notes column open with two notes", "eager", heroSizes %}
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="features" id="features" aria-label="Features">
    <div class="row">
      <div class="row-words">
        <p class="eyebrow">The page</p>
        <h2>It reads like a printed Bible.</h2>
        <p><sup class="verse" aria-hidden="true">1</sup>One chapter at a time, set like a printed page as far as each translation's text allows: paragraphs that indent and poetry that hangs under its first word.</p>
        <p><sup class="verse" aria-hidden="true">2</sup>Four page styles (Printed, Lectern, Study and Classic), in System, Light, Sepia and Dark.</p>
        <p class="more"><a href="{{ '/guide/reader/' | url }}">How the reader works ›</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="row-shot">
        {% shot "reader-lectern-sepia", "Psalm 23 in the Lectern page style on Sepia paper, opening on a large drop numeral, with each line of poetry on its own line", "", rowSizes %}
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row flip">
      <div class="row-words">
        <p class="eyebrow">Marks and notes</p>
        <h2>Pencil in the margin.</h2>
        <p><sup class="verse" aria-hidden="true">1</sup>Highlight a phrase, not only a verse, in colors you choose. Write a note beside it, or bookmark the verse.</p>
        <p><sup class="verse" aria-hidden="true">2</sup>Every delete can be undone.</p>
        <p class="more"><a href="{{ '/guide/highlights/' | url }}">Highlights and bookmarks ›</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="row-shot">
        {% shot "markup-menu", "The markup menu open under verse 5 of John 1, with a row of color swatches above Add Note, Bookmark Verse, Compare Translations, Cross-References and the copy commands", "", rowSizes %}
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row">
      <div class="row-words">
        <p class="eyebrow">The Library</p>
        <h2>People, places and each book's story.</h2>
        <p><sup class="verse" aria-hidden="true">1</sup>Each of the 66 books opens to the people it names, with a family tree on the Mac, a map of its places, and a timeline in the order of history.</p>
        <p class="more"><a href="{{ '/guide/library/' | url }}">The Library ›</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="row-shot">
        {% shot "people", "The People view for Genesis, with Jacob chosen from the list of names and his family drawn as a tree of parents, partners and children", "", rowSizes %}
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row flip">
      <div class="row-words">
        <p class="eyebrow">Search and ⌘K</p>
        <h2>One field finds anything.</h2>
        <p><sup class="verse" aria-hidden="true">1</sup><kbd>⌘K</kbd> on the Mac, and the search button on the iPhone, search the Bible, your notes, highlights, bookmarks and conversations at once.</p>
        <p><sup class="verse" aria-hidden="true">2</sup>On the Mac the same field goes to a passage and runs commands.</p>
        <p class="more"><a href="{{ '/guide/search/' | url }}">Search and ⌘K ›</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="row-shot">
        {% shot "search", "The search field open over John 1 with two words typed and a list of Scripture results with the matched words underlined", "", rowSizes %}
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row">
      <div class="row-words">
        <p class="eyebrow">Reading plans</p>
        <h2>Your group's plan, week by week.</h2>
        <p><sup class="verse" aria-hidden="true">1</sup>A plan is a table of weeks and their readings, for a group that meets weekly. Import one from a text file or a PDF, or type it in.</p>
        <p><sup class="verse" aria-hidden="true">2</sup>Tick a reading when you have read it. Nothing counts as behind.</p>
        <p class="more"><a href="{{ '/guide/reading-plans/' | url }}">Reading plans ›</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="row-shot">
        {% shot "reading-plan", "The reading plan window with five weeks listed down the side and Week 2 chosen, showing its three readings with one ticked", "", rowSizes %}
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row flip">
      <div class="row-words">
        <p class="eyebrow">The study assistant</p>
        <h2>Ask beside the page.</h2>
        <p><sup class="verse" aria-hidden="true">1</sup>Ask about a passage or a chapter in the column beside the reader.</p>
        <p><sup class="verse" aria-hidden="true">2</sup>Apple's on-device model answers by default, on a Mac with Apple Intelligence turned on. Claude, OpenAI or your own endpoint can answer if you add a key.</p>
        <p class="more"><a href="{{ '/guide/assistant/' | url }}">The study assistant ›</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="row-shot">
        {% shot "assistant", "The assistant open beside Psalm 1, with the prompt Name the key themes sent and a numbered reply marked On-device", "", rowSizes %}
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row">
      <div class="row-words">
        <p class="eyebrow">On the iPhone</p>
        <h2>The same desk in your pocket.</h2>
        <p><sup class="verse" aria-hidden="true">1</sup>The iPhone app is the same reader, laid out for the phone. With iCloud sync set up, it shares your notes with the Mac.</p>
        <p class="more"><a href="{{ '/guide/iphone/' | url }}">The iPhone app ›</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="row-shot row-phones">
        {% shot "phone-reader", "The iPhone reader on John 1 with one highlighted phrase and a mark in the right margin for its note, above the Read, Plan, Notes and Ask tabs", "", phoneSizes %}
        {% shot "phone-library", "The iPhone Library on the New Testament tab, with John's chapters under its BibleProject, People, Places and About buttons", "", phoneSizes %}
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row coda">
      <div class="row-words">
        <p class="eyebrow">Keeping what you make</p>
        <h2>Yours to keep.</h2>
        <p><sup class="verse" aria-hidden="true">1</sup>What you make stays on your device. With sync off, as in the build on the <a href="{{ '/build/' | url }}">Build page</a>, it stays on the Mac. If you sign the app with your own iCloud container, it syncs through your private iCloud database.</p>
        <p><sup class="verse" aria-hidden="true">2</sup>A backup is taken once a day, and Restore brings back what the store no longer has. Export All Data writes everything you have made to one file.</p>
        <p class="more"><a href="{{ '/guide/sync-and-backups/' | url }}">Sync and backups ›</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="facts" aria-labelledby="sources-title">
    <p class="eyebrow">Where it comes from</p>
    <h2 id="sources-title">The sources</h2>
    <dl class="aligned">
      <dt>Bible text</dt>
      {#- Public-domain texts lead. The build script downloads all eight (build.md, step 2); none is in the repository. #}
      <dd>{{ credits | namesUnder("Scripture", "Public domain") }}, in the public domain; and {{ credits | namesUnder("Scripture", "Copyright") }}, under their publishers' copyright. <small>· The build script downloads all eight for your own use. The Sources page names each holder.</small></dd>
      {%- set bsb = credits | creditById("bsb") %}
      <dt>Section headings</dt>
      <dd>{{ bsb.name }} <small>· {{ bsb.license }}</small></dd>
      {%- set openbible = credits | creditById("openbible") %}
      <dt>Cross-references</dt>
      <dd>{{ openbible.name }} <small>· {{ openbible.license }}</small></dd>
      {%- set theographic = credits | creditById("theographic") %}
      <dt>People, places and events</dt>
      <dd>{{ theographic.name }} <small>· {{ theographic.license }}</small></dd>
      {%- set literata = credits | creditById("literata") %}
      {%- set sourceSerif = credits | creditById("sourceSerif4") %}
      <dt>Typefaces</dt>
      <dd>{{ literata.name }} and {{ sourceSerif.name }} <small>· {{ literata.license }}</small></dd>
      {%- set grdb = credits | creditById("grdb") %}
      <dt>Software</dt>
      <dd>{{ grdb.name }} <small>· {{ grdb.license }}</small></dd>
    </dl>
    <p class="more"><a href="{{ '/sources/' | url }}">Every source, with its notice and license ›</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="build" aria-labelledby="build-title">
    <p class="eyebrow">Build it</p>
    <h2 id="build-title">Build it yourself.</h2>
    <p>Bible Carrel is not in the App Store. The source is here, and the app builds with Xcode on macOS 26.</p>
    <p class="build-action"><a class="button" href="{{ '/build/' | url }}">Build it from source</a></p>
  </section>
</div>
